  'This course gives journalism students a historically grounded understanding of how the press became a public institution, a business, a profession, a technology system, and a contested democratic force. Students use primary sources and historical cases to understand contemporary debates over press freedom, trust, objectivity, local news, platforms, and accountability. This version adds explicit work on marginalized press traditions, photojournalism, professionalization and objectivity, war and crisis coverage, digital platform history, and future models for local journalism.',
  $jhisjson${"slug": "journalism-history", "title": "Journalism History", "description": "This course gives journalism students a historically grounded understanding of how the press became a public institution, a business, a profession, a technology system, and a contested democratic force. Students use primary sources and historical cases to understand contemporary debates over press freedom, trust, objectivity, local news, platforms, and accountability. This version adds explicit work on marginalized press traditions, photojournalism, professionalization and objectivity, war and crisis coverage, digital platform history, and future models for local journalism.", "topics": [{"id": "17f67266-92cf-4d5b-86c7-53cf00922a65", "type": "topic", "title": "Why Journalism History Matters", "description": "The course begins by treating journalism history as a practical newsroom tool: it helps students recognize recurring conflicts over truth, power, technology, audience, and public trust.", "children": [{"id": "e770ece7-fb05-47c7-8c1d-eedb12e85964", "type": "subtopic", "title": "History as newsroom memory", "children": [{"id": "d5ec47f6-4d15-4d0b-a95e-1e0d4416f5f9", "type": "concept", "title": "recurring patterns"}, {"id": "a1fc202c-98b4-44fc-b1be-3da8a29a0818", "type": "concept", "title": "institutional memory"}, {"id": "4e851c15-d658-48e2-b81c-eaaae817ce13", "type": "concept", "title": "presentism"}], "description": "Students learn why journalists need historical context to avoid treating every media crisis as unprecedented."}, {"id": "a43d3653-bed6-4d92-8237-75ec1e41e47a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public sphere and democratic purpose", "children": [{"id": "bb378afe-1675-4bb3-8f23-df031d7ed4ed", "type": "concept", "title": "watchdog role"}, {"id": "93fb61ef-4993-4dca-bc18-080b635a8c0d", "type": "concept", "title": "public deliberation"}, {"id": "1102805a-8b3b-4bde-b3a5-7942ae89cee4", "type": "concept", "title": "civic information needs"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces the idea that journalism both reflects and shapes public life."}, {"id": "529259fa-d9f4-4ed7-b982-b50c742c2d60", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sources and methods", "children": [{"id": "7c87c208-8935-4d89-9a1c-d49f3db45e2c", "type": "concept", "title": "primary sources"}, {"id": "65ec1076-a74b-44f8-b4a6-3c930f42af38", "type": "concept", "title": "archival context"}, {"id": "ffb61bcb-d66a-41a0-ade2-2517e143bc02", "type": "concept", "title": "source bias"}], "description": "Students practice reading newspapers, editorials, broadcasts, photos, memoirs, archives, and media criticism as historical evidence."}]}, {"id": "66862e9a-cc4b-4394-be2f-83180bfbedf5", "type": "topic", "title": "Colonial and Partisan Press", "description": "This unit shows how early American journalism developed through printers, pamphlets, political conflict, and arguments about liberty before professional objectivity existed.", "children": [{"id": "979550c1-5eb9-4428-bf2e-07543c003f5c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Printers, pamphlets, and public argument", "children": [{"id": "57d127f6-c308-4426-8fb8-a9379a76a3b9", "type": "concept", "title": "broadsides"}, {"id": "3b85cbb0-1a1d-4910-a501-98f649de3387", "type": "concept", "title": "pamphlet culture"}, {"id": "3ff01e0a-1a74-4999-8252-f5328220f204", "type": "concept", "title": "printer-politicians"}], "description": "Students examine how colonial printers created networks of information, dissent, and commercial exchange."}, {"id": "20574855-66e9-4b7a-919e-5c6e8852843a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Partisan newspapers and party politics", "children": [{"id": "66e25f46-21a7-428f-9945-3c99f0f7e3f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalist and Republican papers"}, {"id": "d3ee1921-61f4-47fb-aaac-dedd2cda9c8c", "type": "concept", "title": "patronage"}, {"id": "4d19818b-6e5e-47a4-aa9b-a501da25118b", "type": "concept", "title": "editorial attack"}], "description": "The class studies the openly partisan press as a foundation for political mobilization and factional conflict."}, {"id": "6e3bce85-d60b-467c-a2c1-7af886d1ec21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sedition, dissent, and press liberty", "children": [{"id": "e7bc22b7-651e-447e-ade8-a419ecdfa6e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Zenger tradition"}, {"id": "59bcb18d-e66a-4c5c-be71-a8fbcfd7eca2", "type": "concept", "title": "Sedition Act"}, {"id": "e90c941a-6c87-44e5-b672-6c832d7b9fdb", "type": "concept", "title": "liberty versus order"}], "description": "Students connect early press prosecutions to later First Amendment debates."}]}, {"id": "c0ba187f-7053-42c1-b80e-0837020f1b7d", "type": "topic", "title": "The Penny Press and the Commercial News Revolution", "description": "The penny press changed journalism's audience, economics, and definition of news by making urban, crime, human-interest, and market-driven reporting central to the press.", "children": [{"id": "1134eb97-b32f-46d6-be1f-90c002af70ff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mass circulation and advertising", "children": [{"id": "21c8ba48-2940-42e8-963d-947c697ad820", "type": "concept", "title": "circulation wars"}, {"id": "26a85189-f534-465c-8b31-d3869ae47f31", "type": "concept", "title": "advertising model"}, {"id": "e7af14c2-8756-414c-af76-032b53cd74b8", "type": "concept", "title": "urban readership"}], "description": "Students learn how low prices, street sales, and advertising reshaped who newspapers served."}, {"id": "d6a6dabe-d5ae-49e1-b8ec-af089f35ef58", "type": "subtopic", "title": "News as everyday life", "children": [{"id": "aba19460-6e8f-4fa7-bf72-40d89d297ffa", "type": "concept", "title": "police reporting"}, {"id": "b425fa49-6dd2-463d-b2f5-8c8ca90879cf", "type": "concept", "title": "courts"}, {"id": "d213ff67-daa6-4a01-a8a4-e34439f949d2", "type": "concept", "title": "human-interest stories"}], "description": "The unit tracks the rise of crime, courts, disasters, local events, and human-interest reporting."}, {"id": "288e67c6-1641-40ee-88eb-ef5d0248cb47", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reporters and the birth of news routines", "children": [{"id": "de117325-a3bc-4985-b155-fd02da89fb57", "type": "concept", "title": "beats"}, {"id": "3709f939-bfbe-4088-9635-69b11c9f10ae", "type": "concept", "title": "deadlines"}, {"id": "707acf3c-1df2-4e0e-af60-b6129fb70d97", "type": "concept", "title": "newsroom labor"}], "description": "Students study how beat systems and deadlines created recognizable newsroom practice."}]}, {"id": "fdb0873f-47b1-43d5-8104-585a1057612f", "type": "topic", "title": "Yellow Journalism and Sensationalism", "description": "This unit asks students to separate myth from history: sensational journalism did real harm, but it also drove innovations in visual design, mass readership, and investigative ambition.", "children": [{"id": "09af79e5-d1e2-4b1c-af56-03f2809ef6dc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pulitzer, Hearst, and newspaper competition", "children": [{"id": "1b6eea1b-d979-497d-8cbc-8fbf343ed6d6", "type": "concept", "title": "New York World"}, {"id": "4a19f288-0ffd-48a9-bd07-1223b06a8e15", "type": "concept", "title": "New York Journal"}, {"id": "206b882d-9146-4bf8-9b09-bd1b26779110", "type": "concept", "title": "circulation strategy"}], "description": "Students examine how fierce competition changed headlines, illustrations, stunts, and campaign journalism."}, {"id": "35b5452b-4b34-43f0-b581-97ede843f09c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sensationalism, reform, and war", "children": [{"id": "88ba9603-2ca7-4ebd-9128-19922df571f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Cuba coverage"}, {"id": "17aa9b02-7b8d-4e4b-89e0-248cfea8a1de", "type": "concept", "title": "moral crusades"}, {"id": "7e852a3c-3902-4372-a06c-0a639fd073da", "type": "concept", "title": "war fever"}], "description": "The class evaluates the relationship between sensational coverage, reform campaigns, and the Spanish-American War."}, {"id": "8a12542c-e4f2-4436-8174-93e90e6cc87b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Design and audience innovation", "children": [{"id": "a991fd40-1cd5-4354-9f12-f1d04f8faa8d", "type": "concept", "title": "display type"}, {"id": "d342f4d3-69c9-4796-9722-e636f3c962b8", "type": "concept", "title": "illustration"}, {"id": "d07898c9-42ce-4d88-abff-0ac8d0f97d0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sunday papers"}], "description": "Students analyze how layout, comics, illustrations, and serialized storytelling made newspapers more visual and popular."}]}, {"id": "ce420f3d-0650-4c60-947c-b20376b35cf6", "type": "topic", "title": "Muckraking and the Investigative Tradition", "description": "Muckraking demonstrates journalism's power to expose corruption and abuse while raising enduring questions about evidence, advocacy, reform, and independence.", "children": [{"id": "92177001-e53c-4b2d-80f8-d2477dbbc84a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Progressive Era investigations", "children": [{"id": "0b9f44ea-778c-4110-84d5-7507a0cd2d33", "type": "concept", "title": "Ida Tarbell"}, {"id": "49108030-8101-450f-96fc-be87035a6a39", "type": "concept", "title": "Upton Sinclair"}, {"id": "19321a03-9ed2-4858-8c4e-76b059faba28", "type": "concept", "title": "Lincoln Steffens"}], "description": "Students study landmark investigations into monopolies, labor, food safety, urban poverty, and political corruption."}, {"id": "93495eb3-3110-43f2-8da2-95323fb557ec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence and narrative power", "children": [{"id": "444bb4ac-0508-4be9-b6b4-00634afb9927", "type": "concept", "title": "document trails"}, {"id": "b22574b0-48a4-4694-99ac-0d901cc33d43", "type": "concept", "title": "scene-setting"}, {"id": "e12813e7-c014-4ac6-af16-1e3c40eacdaf", "type": "concept", "title": "public impact"}], "description": "The unit shows how investigative journalism combines documents, observation, interviews, and narrative craft."}, {"id": "b9022eca-0130-46a5-a351-656721c6d5ec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Investigative legacy", "children": [{"id": "77ca2c86-f5a0-49c3-b7e0-b7d552ac1821", "type": "concept", "title": "watchdog reporting"}, {"id": "84f185d5-98e2-4952-9720-36c6940eb8c3", "type": "concept", "title": "FOIA culture"}, {"id": "fa48bbfa-a7c3-4efe-88d8-808d4ce9b1aa", "type": "concept", "title": "nonprofit investigations"}], "description": "Students connect muckraking to later accountability journalism, nonprofit newsrooms, and public records work."}]}, {"id": "7d8acf43-ff42-442d-b993-db7bfb5d94a0", "type": "topic", "title": "The Black Press, Immigrant Press, and Marginalized Publics", "description": "A world-class journalism history course cannot treat the mainstream press as the whole press. This unit centers news institutions built by communities excluded from dominant media power.", "children": [{"id": "d2ea1754-519a-485a-a863-62d401a2f325", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Black press and freedom struggles", "children": [{"id": "ec6fcf7c-149c-4bde-9bf5-3fe227e2d861", "type": "concept", "title": "Freedom's Journal"}, {"id": "d4f0287e-6302-4126-9c30-c3854aeafb22", "type": "concept", "title": "anti-lynching campaigns"}, {"id": "3b319f4d-97cb-4a00-96d4-138a6bbdc568", "type": "concept", "title": "Chicago Defender"}], "description": "Students learn how Black newspapers documented violence, built political identity, and challenged national hypocrisy."}, {"id": "62f808e7-e710-4d65-bade-09e0b9e85139", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Immigrant and ethnic-language newspapers", "children": [{"id": "53aa20a4-f3d3-42ee-857d-8a41c837a574", "type": "concept", "title": "German-language press"}, {"id": "9fe7878b-5f1b-47ca-9c11-43f73cfecbc6", "type": "concept", "title": "Spanish-language press"}, {"id": "ffe422bb-d739-4c60-ba2d-1a3e2b01dbff", "type": "concept", "title": "community information"}], "description": "The class studies how immigrant communities used newspapers for language, labor, politics, mutual aid, and identity."}, {"id": "d848eced-188d-41e5-92eb-e9d158c2837b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigenous, labor, feminist, and LGBTQ press traditions", "children": [{"id": "21f345f2-0b5b-4776-99cc-c20e8eb457fd", "type": "concept", "title": "movement journalism"}, {"id": "e4220f7c-afe1-40b6-b8fd-2dd68ee12bb3", "type": "concept", "title": "community accountability"}, {"id": "0ae37c57-3b9a-47b9-b43b-13f09dbef6a5", "type": "concept", "title": "counterpublics"}], "description": "Students examine alternative and movement presses as institutions of survival, advocacy, and critique."}]}, {"id": "9b3c6ad5-8db4-4349-ba2e-cf37fc879197", "type": "topic", "title": "Professionalization, Objectivity, and Journalism Education", "description": "This unit explores how journalism became a profession with schools, codes, norms, and claims to objectivity, while also questioning whose values those norms served.", "children": [{"id": "fa691be8-4e14-4997-89e7-3e301aa9abd2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rise of journalism schools and professional norms", "children": [{"id": "0081bb9a-c772-4049-be9a-153e0daa54bb", "type": "concept", "title": "journalism schools"}, {"id": "b9b49608-06e3-4e88-a0f9-2a6a56af56d6", "type": "concept", "title": "press clubs"}, {"id": "1bcbd6b9-e409-4d5b-90ef-2467a4642500", "type": "concept", "title": "professional identity"}], "description": "Students trace the growth of formal training, press associations, and shared standards."}, {"id": "21401e98-2cfe-46ee-8eaa-71176962ce19", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Objectivity as history", "children": [{"id": "d086f2c1-9e5a-4783-baa7-0e596a26176e", "type": "concept", "title": "strategic ritual"}, {"id": "e3403b62-6e47-4334-be27-d2602aeb01e7", "type": "concept", "title": "verification"}, {"id": "f364b896-615d-4518-8750-85fb629be4e7", "type": "concept", "title": "neutrality debates"}], "description": "The unit treats objectivity as a historical response to partisanship, propaganda, public relations, and business pressures."}, {"id": "b73f79b9-a517-4256-913b-29cc5afbb2bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Wire services and standardization", "children": [{"id": "54006f2b-eeab-4798-97f5-851e58fc6026", "type": "concept", "title": "inverted pyramid"}, {"id": "9ddcc474-e41b-4b37-be87-eb58f9b7f04d", "type": "concept", "title": "wire style"}, {"id": "9f9e0f86-7097-4645-a829-211e35e6e527", "type": "concept", "title": "national distribution"}], "description": "Students learn how AP, UPI, and wire copy shaped speed, style, news values, and national news agendas."}]}, {"id": "f4ff9148-2dd1-488e-8634-cddb63490701", "type": "topic", "title": "Photojournalism, Documentary, and Visual Evidence", "description": "Visual journalism changed how audiences understood war, poverty, disaster, protest, and everyday life. Students learn to read images as both evidence and editorial construction.", "children": [{"id": "904626af-ecfa-4e26-be0b-f02813e6b955", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early photography and illustrated news", "children": [{"id": "1dec9149-70fb-45c3-a43d-a4b108cef061", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil War photography"}, {"id": "c2b30f04-f53c-4e41-9fea-fd71939d8d11", "type": "concept", "title": "halftone printing"}, {"id": "7ca6c3ad-8e44-45d1-9ac3-6aa1b30f27b6", "type": "concept", "title": "illustrated weeklies"}], "description": "Students trace the move from engravings and illustrated papers to photographic news evidence."}, {"id": "933c9d1d-e1de-47a5-89cd-9ab5559d41e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Documentary photography and social reform", "children": [{"id": "58c62df0-e1b6-46e6-a6dc-dccdefb786d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Jacob Riis"}, {"id": "d0f09d20-adf4-4283-824b-a36be0d6ff73", "type": "concept", "title": "Lewis Hine"}, {"id": "eb4a2f81-fbb6-46c8-a918-b6c8bd085672", "type": "concept", "title": "Farm Security Administration"}], "description": "The unit studies visual reform traditions and the ethics of representing poverty, labor, and suffering."}, {"id": "c4ce1fee-ec14-491f-9544-47e8d50dbe01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Television images and public memory", "children": [{"id": "06f470b1-a2ff-4b3b-aeda-54dd9335b46c", "type": "concept", "title": "iconic photographs"}, {"id": "b06ba412-939c-406f-b2f7-383d8df21147", "type": "concept", "title": "visual framing"}, {"id": "ec7a2313-cd6f-40ca-86d0-8698fc5dbab2", "type": "concept", "title": "caption context"}], "description": "Students examine how iconic images can shape collective memory while simplifying complex events."}]}, {"id": "da48e72d-fb7d-428d-b043-4dbdf20240f2", "type": "topic", "title": "The Rise of Broadcast Journalism", "description": "Radio and television transformed journalism's speed, intimacy, authority, and relationship to national public life.", "children": [{"id": "93df923c-0388-4a86-82e9-64228b4981dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Radio news and live national audience", "children": [{"id": "fb318819-69a5-42fa-8129-e40e140ffa35", "type": "concept", "title": "fireside chats"}, {"id": "cadde6d3-4cce-43d7-8ac8-2a61417d7f3d", "type": "concept", "title": "war reporting"}, {"id": "051adbcf-b1bd-4e14-b1c4-7fb83886f63b", "type": "concept", "title": "network news"}], "description": "Students learn how radio created immediacy, voice authority, and shared national listening experiences."}, {"id": "04fcf2fc-a573-43d2-b776-b67774ab5c4e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Television news and the anchor era", "children": [{"id": "3907503f-98ca-4230-9d36-edc3d0b0120b", "type": "concept", "title": "network anchors"}, {"id": "b880708f-c620-4d09-86e1-9f4de4463267", "type": "concept", "title": "evening news"}, {"id": "941ad9e8-ada2-4b53-954e-fd4fb13fd4c6", "type": "concept", "title": "visual credibility"}], "description": "The class studies how television changed political communication, crisis coverage, and trust in journalists."}, {"id": "1584eee1-ffd3-4bf5-b72b-295ee0b33ad3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regulation and public-interest broadcasting", "children": [{"id": "9b7c74b7-4d26-4957-ac93-2d7e99082f10", "type": "concept", "title": "FCC"}, {"id": "c9e356b6-e699-46af-8bf9-60f1c50c4739", "type": "concept", "title": "Fairness Doctrine"}, {"id": "68703255-16b6-4d95-a4b8-fab9cf940d2b", "type": "concept", "title": "public media"}], "description": "Students connect broadcast history to spectrum scarcity, FCC regulation, fairness debates, and public broadcasting."}]}, {"id": "3c4a556d-cbfe-4e55-b230-0979f56c13f7", "type": "topic", "title": "The Press, War, and Crisis", "description": "War and crisis reporting reveal the tension between national security, propaganda, censorship, access, trauma, and the public's right to know.", "children": [{"id": "6d975ac8-d53f-4a1a-88ac-b583bfcd93f7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "War correspondents and censorship", "children": [{"id": "6fd0dd21-4206-4024-a9c5-773a0b0b995f", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil War correspondents"}, {"id": "b8740a9c-3825-4a00-be53-897b7b8f4028", "type": "concept", "title": "World War II pools"}, {"id": "c6bb8359-feff-4b6d-96b9-fc430858616e", "type": "concept", "title": "military censorship"}], "description": "Students compare press access and censorship across major conflicts."}, {"id": "67f3545a-2a78-4e0f-879d-8ec0d869d792", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vietnam and credibility", "children": [{"id": "b76c8c59-72ac-40fb-9e15-031a0cf9174d", "type": "concept", "title": "credibility gap"}, {"id": "e6c94de2-3679-4c7e-b917-1156f928c539", "type": "concept", "title": "body counts"}, {"id": "47ba26b3-98e1-4234-844b-9b0e4a59b9ef", "type": "concept", "title": "living-room war"}], "description": "The unit explores how television, official credibility gaps, and battlefield reporting shaped public debate."}, {"id": "9806ad12-9759-4beb-9804-138622c59263", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Crisis, disaster, and terror coverage", "children": [{"id": "c80c94ca-5915-476f-a759-4d1b7474030f", "type": "concept", "title": "breaking-news verification"}, {"id": "db376195-9dae-49a9-8685-e8718e4d0272", "type": "concept", "title": "trauma coverage"}, {"id": "e89d2516-335c-4b56-bf45-e09eba272e58", "type": "concept", "title": "official sources"}], "description": "Students study how journalists cover emergencies while avoiding rumor, panic, and dehumanization."}]}, {"id": "57feb442-3d37-4832-9d44-8f30ab35c8d0", "type": "topic", "title": "The Press and Civil Rights", "description": "Civil rights coverage shows journalism at its best and worst: exposing injustice, amplifying protest, ignoring communities, or reproducing dominant power.", "children": [{"id": "3d039437-331e-4bea-9985-4b4966d9ac86", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mainstream coverage of civil rights", "children": [{"id": "76c26974-4112-4056-a41b-00b85c18f3ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Birmingham"}, {"id": "c6d6dc76-7bcd-471e-be7a-ac51db2c44a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Selma"}, {"id": "53e75f23-0a2e-4bdb-b275-2ae223e2f021", "type": "concept", "title": "television news"}], "description": "Students analyze how national attention changed when violence and protest became visible to broad audiences."}, {"id": "9c81b4b1-6bb9-4de0-b6f1-3d0432d13453", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Movement media and Black journalists", "children": [{"id": "32aa5501-9d63-46be-8a5b-20939261fc6b", "type": "concept", "title": "Black correspondents"}, {"id": "f3494737-b70f-46af-9279-90c60d033f95", "type": "concept", "title": "movement newsletters"}, {"id": "ff22a23b-77b6-4c5f-8394-afef865cc094", "type": "concept", "title": "community papers"}], "description": "The unit centers reporters and publications that documented the movement from inside affected communities."}, {"id": "365d23e9-a3a1-4f81-8446-596c6f0943a2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Framing protest and power", "children": [{"id": "3f89c8c9-9f76-4a7d-a566-9f3a6f9cbbdc", "type": "concept", "title": "law-and-order framing"}, {"id": "39271124-4238-4fd0-b4fa-43c3011f9322", "type": "concept", "title": "respectability"}, {"id": "a8cc2eb9-b90a-4246-861d-45f819f027bc", "type": "concept", "title": "source selection"}], "description": "Students learn to identify frames that criminalize protest, erase causes, or shift attention from injustice to disorder."}]}, {"id": "aebc93ca-af19-403a-a579-e91fc2d7c102", "type": "topic", "title": "Watergate and Accountability Journalism", "description": "Watergate is useful not as a myth of heroic journalism, but as a case study in sourcing, institutional support, documents, legal context, and political accountability.", "children": [{"id": "9fb8c34b-7445-497f-86ce-208e95a3f4dc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reporting the Watergate story", "children": [{"id": "cd0ab392-8005-4a4c-b55d-998b81647093", "type": "concept", "title": "anonymous sources"}, {"id": "b3ab2af1-b07c-4ca7-ab65-69c606c5275e", "type": "concept", "title": "follow-the-money reporting"}, {"id": "38bd64da-a701-4e8e-8328-fcbc3b08a5eb", "type": "concept", "title": "editorial support"}], "description": "Students study how patient sourcing, documentary evidence, and editorial backing turned a break-in into a constitutional crisis."}, {"id": "1c4ba0ea-2b8f-4112-8ef9-a10de46acbf8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Myth and reality of watchdog journalism", "children": [{"id": "8eb8652e-dda0-48f2-b7da-c640084ee199", "type": "concept", "title": "institutional checks"}, {"id": "a251b339-e848-40e2-9c15-8e1a380c78d7", "type": "concept", "title": "hero narrative"}, {"id": "1a8a169d-b18d-40e2-ab49-d65d1163846f", "type": "concept", "title": "source dependence"}], "description": "The class evaluates what Watergate obscures about courts, Congress, whistleblowers, and collaborative accountability."}, {"id": "6350304b-ee1a-49a0-9f30-26193368cc30", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Investigative norms after Watergate", "children": [{"id": "987fb87b-3bbe-4bd8-a082-219ee023d670", "type": "concept", "title": "investigative desks"}, {"id": "741c8f83-94fc-42c1-b45a-8e56f7c45fb9", "type": "concept", "title": "leak culture"}, {"id": "dda71bfd-391a-4d99-b6e8-1d028097c444", "type": "concept", "title": "public trust"}], "description": "Students connect Watergate to later investigative teams, source practices, and skepticism toward government power."}]}, {"id": "10bc1151-387e-42db-b85a-155eaf82d42d", "type": "topic", "title": "The Internet's Disruption and Platform Era", "description": "Digital media reorganized journalism's economics, distribution, speed, audience relationship, and authority.", "children": [{"id": "6d4c2d65-37a5-4557-b8f0-1cc03abe2e01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "From web publishing to social platforms", "children": [{"id": "35bb5e4b-d1d3-4fe7-a434-6b9260ddcacb", "type": "concept", "title": "web-first publishing"}, {"id": "22c3a015-cd98-41af-a21c-f628960999c1", "type": "concept", "title": "search"}, {"id": "48af2941-926a-4ef4-8aeb-f85c6bcdf01c", "type": "concept", "title": "social distribution"}], "description": "Students trace the move from newspaper websites to search, social, newsletters, podcasts, and platform-dependent distribution."}, {"id": "f3756474-96db-41f7-83c1-77af72a5a38a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Blogging, participatory media, and citizen journalism", "children": [{"id": "4757f381-098b-4bc2-b3cc-a59b10bd1b1e", "type": "concept", "title": "blogs"}, {"id": "c7a8db90-b648-4c99-b5e4-03dc550af82e", "type": "concept", "title": "user-generated content"}, {"id": "cbc04d3f-4c8a-4dee-b503-a937ed84e299", "type": "concept", "title": "networked publics"}], "description": "The unit examines how nontraditional publishers challenged gatekeeping and expanded public voice."}, {"id": "6ebcbbf1-51d1-4abb-b52b-2215655e764d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Misinformation, metrics, and trust", "children": [{"id": "fc7704c6-2411-4e0c-8ebb-c1814fdc807d", "type": "concept", "title": "click metrics"}, {"id": "02e788b4-57f2-48f7-8b9e-1e19ef88aaff", "type": "concept", "title": "misinformation"}, {"id": "1f72e036-4032-427f-9ce2-6d5aaf34b2bc", "type": "concept", "title": "trust repair"}], "description": "Students study how speed, analytics, virality, and platform incentives create new historical pressures on accuracy and trust."}]}, {"id": "018a08a9-719b-4226-85ad-8c75ed76b9fa", "type": "topic", "title": "Decline of Local News and the Future of Journalism", "description": "The closing unit connects history to the present crisis of local news, showing students that business models, ownership, labor, community trust, and public policy are historical questions.", "children": [{"id": "a293a820-f25f-4d65-ba84-292710436a92", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Local news economics and ownership", "children": [{"id": "4bdf89bd-4443-465a-970c-923bca186f8f", "type": "concept", "title": "classified ads"}, {"id": "b7cd4e35-7e75-4c06-bdd6-8c4813519057", "type": "concept", "title": "chain ownership"}, {"id": "9a09717c-4612-4894-b7fc-f385e4045f0f", "type": "concept", "title": "news deserts"}], "description": "Students learn how advertising collapse, consolidation, hedge-fund ownership, and platform capture weakened local journalism."}, {"id": "62963d23-8265-4796-b088-95495d5ff218", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Community information needs", "children": [{"id": "a6eb862f-f7d6-41d6-8f8a-41bfc851a101", "type": "concept", "title": "civic data"}, {"id": "8c6d7511-65da-4c60-a0d9-8c1177efc9df", "type": "concept", "title": "local accountability"}, {"id": "71705fcf-b2e6-4b4b-bb99-0a5da8b24444", "type": "concept", "title": "information inequity"}], "description": "The class studies what communities lose when local reporting disappears and what alternatives are emerging."}, {"id": "2904ef7f-0ab6-42db-80ec-20cfddfbd9b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New models and press freedom futures", "children": [{"id": "a54ddb60-3e59-493b-b806-2f1186fac108", "type": "concept", "title": "nonprofit news"}, {"id": "8bd8983e-90cb-414c-af4e-dd0dc5078d68", "type": "concept", "title": "public funding"}, {"id": "33d0107f-0319-4937-9cba-a411f14f9a4c", "type": "concept", "title": "community ownership"}], "description": "Students evaluate nonprofit, public, cooperative, university, startup, and policy-supported journalism models."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$jhisjson$::jsonb,
